course: dictionary
lesson: 5015
title: Inflatable: full of air
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: An inflatable is something you fill with air or gas so that it gets bigger. The word comes from «inflate», which means to fill with air. «An inflatable boat» is a boat you blow up before you use it.
explain: Inflatable can be an adjective or a noun. In «an inflatable castle», inflatable describes the castle. In «the inflatables on the beach», the word is a noun for the blow-up toys and rings themselves.
explain: Context clues warn you about danger. «Wind blowing off the land can push inflatables far out to sea in minutes.» The words far out to sea and in minutes show that they can drift away very fast, so you must be careful.
explain: Some inflatables look safe but do not protect you. «Inflatable armbands and pool toys are not life-saving devices.» Armbands help you float, but they are not life jackets. Use inflatables only in shallow water, near an adult.
fact: Inflatable means able to be filled with air or gas to make it bigger.
fact: «An inflatable boat» is a boat that you fill with air before use.
fact: Inflatable can be an adjective, as in «an inflatable castle», or a noun, as in «inflatables».
fact: Air pressure inside keeps an inflatable firm. If the air leaks out, it goes soft.
fact: Inflatable toys can blow away in wind or float out to sea. At the coast, if someone is in trouble, call 999 and ask for the Coastguard.
exercise: «The children took an inflatable boat to the lake.» What is an inflatable? | A rope for tying boats up | *Something you can fill with air to make it bigger | A fish that lives in lakes | A heavy metal boat that sinks
exercise: «Wind blowing off the land can push inflatables far out to sea in minutes.» What does this sentence warn you about? | Inflatables always stay safe on the sand | Inflatables are only for sunny days | *Inflatables can be blown far out to sea very quickly | Wind makes inflatables go soft and flat
exercise: Which word in «an inflatable castle» tells you what kind of castle it is? | an | *inflatable | castle
test: «Inflatable armbands and pool toys are not life-saving devices.» Can you rely on armbands to keep you safe in deep water? | No, because they are only for bath time | Yes, if they are bright and blue | *No, they help you float but they are not life jackets | Yes, they always keep you safe
test: Where should children use inflatable toys? | In deep, open sea with no adult near | In fast-flowing rivers alone | *In shallow water, with an adult close by | On a cliff top when it is very windy
exam: Why does an inflatable stay firm and round? | *Air pressure inside keeps it firm | Paint on the outside holds its shape | It is made of hard wood under the plastic | The sun dries it into shape
